    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.completion\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.11 (!at least latest Nightly build!)\n\n### Description\n\nWhen the parameter label returned by the language server is a substring of the function name, that part of the function name will be highlighted instead of the parameter.\n\nIn the attached screenshot you can see the signature help from mini.completion and the nvim built-in version that works as expected. Looking at the code, the built in one searches for string matches starting from the first trigger character instead of from the beginning of the signature.\n\n<img width=\"352\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cc1804bf-db29-44b3-b6a3-b6d5f866166f\" />\n\n### Reproduction\n\nThis is using the `fennel-ls` language server (note that the signature help feature is only available in the latest git version of fennel-ls), but I think the bug is independent of the specific LS, it's just that fennel-ls doesn't add any type annotations to the parameter name so a substring match is more likely.\n\nThis is the code used:\n```\n(fn path-split [path sep] {})\n(path-split [cursor here])\n```\n\nThis is the response sent by the language server when the signature help is requested:\n\n```\n[DEBUG][2025-04-04 11:41:12] .../vim/lsp/rpc.lua:391    \"rpc.receive\"   {\n  id = 47,\n  jsonrpc = \"2.0\",\n  result = {\n    activeParameter = 0,\n    activeSignature = 0,\n    signatures = { {\n        label = \"(path-split path sep)\",\n        parameters = { {\n            label = \"path\"\n          }, {\n            label = \"sep\"\n          } }\n      } }\n  }\n}```",
    "closed_at": "2025-04-04T12:28:15Z",
